{
  "conversation_lines": [
    {
      "turn_number": 1,
      "time": "2196-03-24 07:15:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Good morning. I’m Dr. Lee, one of the hospitalists. You were transferred here because of chest pain and trouble breathing. Can you tell me what happened?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 2,
      "time": "2196-03-24 07:17:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "Yes. Last night around 6 PM, I suddenly had sharp pain in my right side and back when I breathed in. I couldn’t lie flat and got short of breath just walking to the bathroom."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 3,
      "time": "2196-03-24 07:19:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "I see. You mentioned it felt like your old pulmonary embolism. Does it feel like your heart attacks too?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 4,
      "time": "2196-03-24 07:20:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "No, not at all. My heart attacks were pressure in the center, going to my arm and jaw. This is different—only hurts when I breathe."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 5,
      "time": "2196-03-24 07:22:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "That helps. You flew last week, which raises concern for a blood clot. At the other hospital, they started you on heparin just in case."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 6,
      "time": "2196-03-24 07:23:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "Yes, they said it was precautionary. But I haven’t had any leg swelling or pain."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 7,
      "time": "2196-03-24 07:25:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "That’s reassuring. We’ve reviewed your chest X-ray—it’s clear, no pneumonia or fluid. Your heart rhythm and oxygen levels are stable."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 8,
      "time": "2196-03-24 07:27:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "So it’s not my heart or lungs infected?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 9,
      "time": "2196-03-24 07:28:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "No signs of infection. We’re still concerned about a blood clot in the lungs, but we have to be careful with testing because of your kidney transplant."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 10,
      "time": "2196-03-24 07:30:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "Right, contrast can hurt the kidney. What are we doing instead?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 11,
      "time": "2196-03-24 07:32:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "We ordered a V/Q scan first—it uses less contrast. And we’ll check your blood work, including kidney function and troponin to rule out heart injury."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 12,
      "time": "2196-03-24 12:50:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Hi again. Your V/Q scan came back—very low chance of a pulmonary embolism."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 13,
      "time": "2196-03-24 12:52:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "That’s good, right?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 14,
      "time": "2196-03-24 12:54:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Yes, very. But because your symptoms are similar to your prior PE and you’re at higher risk, we’re going to do a CT scan with contrast, but only after giving you IV fluids before and after to protect your kidney."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 15,
      "time": "2196-03-24 12:56:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "Okay, that makes sense. Will it hurt my transplant?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 16,
      "time": "2196-03-24 12:58:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "We’re taking every precaution. Hydration helps flush the contrast. Your baseline creatinine is 1.8, which is stable, so we expect it to bounce back."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 17,
      "time": "2196-03-25 10:30:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Good morning. You had the CT scan this morning. I have the results—no blood clot in your lungs, and no aortic tear. Great news."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 18,
      "time": "2196-03-25 10:32:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "Oh, thank you. So what’s causing the pain?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 19,
      "time": "2196-03-25 10:34:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "It’s likely pleuritic pain—irritation of the lining around the lung. Could be from a small area of atelectasis, which we saw on the scan. It often improves with time and deep breathing."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 20,
      "time": "2196-03-25 10:36:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "And my heart? You said troponins were normal?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 21,
      "time": "2196-03-25 10:38:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Yes, three sets of troponins were negative, and your EKG hasn’t changed. So no sign of a heart attack."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 22,
      "time": "2196-03-25 10:40:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "Good. What about my kidney? I know the CT used contrast."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 23,
      "time": "2196-03-25 10:42:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Your creatinine went up to 2.3 from 1.8. That’s expected after contrast. We’ll keep you hydrated, and it should come down. You’ll need labs in a few days to check it."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 24,
      "time": "2196-03-25 10:44:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "Okay, I’ll follow up. What about my transplant meds?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 25,
      "time": "2196-03-25 10:46:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Your tacrolimus level was 11.6—slightly high. We’re adjusting your dose from 2 mg twice a day to 1.5 mg to keep it safe."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 26,
      "time": "2196-03-25 10:48:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "I did take a double dose by mistake last week. Is that why?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 27,
      "time": "2196-03-25 10:50:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "That could explain it. We’ll monitor the level and adjust as needed. Sirolimus stays the same."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 28,
      "time": "2196-03-25 15:20:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Just checking in. How’s the chest pain today?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 29,
      "time": "2196-03-25 15:22:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "Better. Still a twinge when I take a deep breath, but much less than yesterday."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 30,
      "time": "2196-03-25 15:24:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "That’s great progress. We’re planning to discharge you tomorrow morning if you keep improving."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 31,
      "time": "2196-03-25 15:26:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "What do I need to do after I go home?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 32,
      "time": "2196-03-25 15:28:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "First, call your cardiologist in the next week or two to schedule a stress test or echo. Even though this wasn’t a heart issue, we want to make sure everything’s stable."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 33,
      "time": "2196-03-25 15:30:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "Okay, I’ll call them soon."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 34,
      "time": "2196-03-25 15:32:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Second, get your kidney labs done in the next few days. Your transplant team will review them to monitor the creatinine and your med levels."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 35,
      "time": "2196-03-25 15:34:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "Got it. And I’m still on the same heart and blood pressure meds?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 36,
      "time": "2196-03-25 15:36:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Yes—amlodipine, metoprolol, aspirin, clopidogrel, and atorvastatin all stay the same. Keep taking furosemide for heart failure too."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 37,
      "time": "2196-03-25 15:38:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "And my gout and reflux meds?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 38,
      "time": "2196-03-25 15:40:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Yes, continue febuxostat and pantoprazole. We’ll send you home with updated prescriptions, including the new tacrolimus dose."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 39,
      "time": "2196-03-26 08:10:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Good morning. You’re doing well. We’re clearing you to go home today."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 40,
      "time": "2196-03-26 08:12:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "That’s great. Just to confirm—I call cardiology, get labs done, and take the lower tacrolimus dose?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 41,
      "time": "2196-03-26 08:14:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Exactly. And use the nitroglycerin only if you have squeezing chest pain, not the kind you had this time. If the pain changes or worsens, come back right away."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 42,
      "time": "2196-03-26 08:16:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "Understood. Thank you for taking such good care of me."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 43,
      "time": "2196-03-26 08:18:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "You’re very welcome. Take care, and we’ll see you only in follow-up!"
    }
  ],
  "summary": {
    "admission_start": "2196-03-24 06:57:00",
    "admission_end": "2196-03-26 19:20:00",
    "summary_paragraph": "A woman with a history of kidney transplant, coronary artery disease, prior pulmonary embolism, and heart failure presented with sudden-onset pleuritic chest pain and dyspnea, concerning for pulmonary embolism. She was transferred on empiric heparin. Initial chest X-ray was clear, and troponins were negative. A V/Q scan showed very low likelihood of pulmonary embolism, followed by a contrast CT angiogram that confirmed no embolism or aortic abnormality. Her chest pain improved during hospitalization. Creatinine rose from 1.8 to 2.3 likely due to contrast exposure, consistent with acute kidney injury. Tacrolimus dose was reduced from 2 mg to 1.5 mg twice daily based on therapeutic drug levels. She was discharged home with instructions to follow up with cardiology for a stress test or echocardiogram and to monitor kidney function with outpatient labs.",
    "problems": [
      "Other chest pain",
      "Acute kidney failure, unspecified",
      "Heart failure, unspecified",
      "Kidney transplant status",
      "Atherosclerotic heart disease of native coronary artery without angina pectoris",
      "Personal history of pulmonary embolism",
      "Old myocardial infarction",
      "Essential (primary) hypertension",
      "Gout, unspecified",
      "Gastro-esophageal reflux disease without esophagitis"
    ]
  }
}
